    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm having some trouble with an iam policy. i'm trying to let a lambda function write logs to a specific s3 bucket but i keep getting an access denied error. i thought i had the permissions right but it's still failing. can u take a look at this json and see what i'm missing?\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-company-logs-bucket\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your IAM policy statement looks syntactically correct, so the issue likely stems from the specific ARN format required for Lambda execution roles rather than the policy version itself. While \"s3:PutObject\" on the bucket ARN is generally correct, you must ensure your Lambda execution role includes the explicit `arn:aws:s3:::my-company-logs-bucket` resource as a well-known identifier. Additionally, verify that the Lambda function's VPC configuration or KMS key settings aren't inadvertently blocking S3 access, and confirm the bucket owner matches the environment where the Lambda is running.",
