    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "oh wait i think i see what i did wrong. i didn't include the /* at the end of the resource line. i thought s3:PutObject just needed the bucket itself but i guess it needs the actual objects inside it. \n\nso if i change the resource to \"arn:aws:s3:::my-company-logs-bucket/*\" should that fix the access denied? also is there anything else that could be blocking it? like maybe a bucket policy or something on the s3 side?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, adding the `/*` is the correct fix because you must explicitly grant permissions for the objects inside the bucket rather than the bucket itself; the correct resource should be `arn:aws:s3:::my-company-logs-bucket/*`. Additionally, you should verify your S3 bucket policy is configured to explicitly allow read/write permissions to that same Lambda or execution role IAM principal. Finally, ensure the Access Control List (ACL) on the bucket's default objects allows your Lambda to write to them upon creation.",
      "source": "target",
